const body = `<h1>3.4b — String Comparison: Password &amp; Command Checker</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Basic Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Build: Password Validator</h3>
        <p>Yesterday you learned that <code>==</code> doesn't work for comparing String <em>contents</em> — you need <code>.equals()</code>. Today you'll build a password checker that also handles case-insensitive matching.</p>
        <p>Create a class called <code>PasswordChecker</code>. Hardcode a <code>String userInput</code> and a <code>String correctPassword</code> (set to "DragonFire42").</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Use <code>.equals()</code> to check for an exact match. Print "Access granted" or "Access denied".</li>
            <li>Add a check using <code>.equalsIgnoreCase()</code> — if the user typed the right letters but wrong case, print "Check your caps lock!"</li>
            <li>Add a length check: if the input is shorter than 8 characters, print "Password too short."</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
        <h3>Trap to Discover</h3>
        <p>Try comparing with <code>==</code> instead of <code>.equals()</code>. Make userInput = "DragonFire42" (same text). What happens? Now make userInput = new String("DragonFire42"). What happens now? Why are they different?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/comparing-objects.html">Comparing Objects</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">String Methods</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
